 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483344 #edb/ref 1085 "Orlando wants to prepare and use liquid fertilizer on a ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ hectare plot of land located on his farm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483344 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "09bfd754-ec9d-5f97-ba14-6b16906c1f3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483344 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186483507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a single fraction, the exact area of the plot of land is$\\,${{field-1}}\nhectares." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "319f568f-1d7f-56e6-aaee-eed8bdcd4507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483357 #edb/ref 1074 "The exact area of land is ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ hectares.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{7}{9}\\times \\frac{7}{9}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\times 7}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{7}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{49}{81}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1071 "Orlando bought $6$ packages of powdered fertilizer, for which he paid $$96.44.$ Estimate the price of each package by rounding the amount of money he spent to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each package is $\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5606b628-dd5a-51e3-a0c0-f4f6b8db27d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483369 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the price of each package, we need to find an approximation to $96.44÷6.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n96.44\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $6$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷6=16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each package is $$16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1071 "To prepare liquid fertilizer, $5.5$ grams of fertilizer powder should be dissolved in a liter of water. How much powder must be dissolved in $8.6$ liters of water to make fertilizer?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$grams of powder must be dissolved in $8.6$ liters of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d4461c0-b1a3-50ea-9b6f-7279220b9f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483372 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of powder that must be dissolved in $8.6$ liters of water, we have to multiply $8.6$ by $5.5.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{3} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $8.6$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $5.5.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $4730$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n47\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{30}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $8.6\\times 5.5=47.30=47.3.$\n\nWe conclude that $47.3$ grams of powder must be dissolved in $8.6$ liters of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1071 "Orlando used $0.3\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer on Monday morning. He then used $0.18\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer on Monday afternoon. How much powder did Orlando use that day?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, Orlando used$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88140710-3cf2-5db3-9844-9b23db7c5cfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483385 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of powder Orlando used, we need to calculate the sum $0.3+0.18.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.3$ and $2$ decimal places in $0.18.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0.3=0.30\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Orlando used $0.48\\,\\text{kg}$ of powdered fertilizer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1071 "The package of powdered fertilizer that Orlando opened before starting work on Monday weighed $0.95\\,\\text{kg}.$ How much fertilizer was left in the package after he'd finished on Monday afternoon?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, there was$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{kg}$ of fertilizer left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5ff9fd7-f4d9-5987-9375-7f097e607931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483416 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 3, we find that Orlando used $0.48\\,\\text{kg}$ of fertilizer on Monday.\n\nTo determine the amount of fertilizer left in the package, we need to calculate $0.95-0.48.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.47\\,\\text{kg}$ of fertilizer remained." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1071 "During a particular weekend, Orlando made $10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of liquid fertilizer. He used $9\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ liters from this amount. How much liquid fertilizer did Orlando have left over? Express your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.\n\nOrlando had$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of liquid fertilizer left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e29d8681-5a80-5808-b535-ab9dc2e4503c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483420 #edb/ref 1074 "Orlando had $(10\\,\\frac{1}{2}-9\\,\\frac{1}{6})$ liters of liquid fertilizer left over.\n\nWriting $10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n10\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{10\\times 2+1}{2}=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nWriting $9\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n9\\,\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{9\\times 6+1}{6}=\\frac{55}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{55}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $6.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{21}{2}$ over a denominator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=\\frac{21\\times 3}{2\\times 3}=\\frac{63}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{6}-\\frac{55}{6}=\\frac{8}{6}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{8÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Orlando had $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters of liquid fertilizer left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483436 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483436 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0dea133d-38d7-5e9d-be97-1523a15613ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483436 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483478 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77ccb2b4-8521-5fb0-838a-8d9e733377c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483478 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483484 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483484 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a40799f7-5820-5197-8386-13a490766fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483484 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483489 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afa77968-6d53-5517-8e75-865f5e57004e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483489 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483498 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483498 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2f42671-6e27-5767-8033-1903c6c14426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483498 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483500 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483500 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da388f04-2c89-564e-9098-bf3330aa1b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483500 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9dfbbaa-32ec-59db-9c4a-af4215cd2286" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483357 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
